    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d175">

                <pb facs="0367" n="241" sameAs="frus:frus1925v01/pg241"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">121.5764/1</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in Hungary</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Brentano</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Budapest</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1925-07-18"><hi rendition="#italic">July 18,
                        1925</hi></date>.<lb/>[Received August 3.]</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 680</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: With reference to the Department’s
                    instruction No. 940 of April 21, 1925,<anchor n="65" xml:id="d175-f1"/>
                    concerning agents of the Customs Division of the United States Treasury
                    stationed abroad being regularly and officially attached to American Diplomatic
                    Missions, I have the honor to transmit a copy of a note from the Foreign Office
                    dated July 8, 1925.<anchor n="66" xml:id="d175-f2"/> received yesterday, stating
                    that Mr. James F. O’Neill, Customs Attaché, is placed on the Hungarian
                    diplomatic list. Mr. O’Neill has been informed of this action.</p>
                <p>I have [etc.]</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Theodore Brentano</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
